window.PHOTO_ROUND = {"title":"Playbook shared photography concept round","order":["entry-43","entry-76","entry-12","entry-58","entry-27"],"participants":[{"id":"entry-12","model":"gpt-6-astra","label":"GPT-6 Astra","requestedReasoning":"high"},{"id":"entry-27","model":"gpt-5.6-sol","label":"GPT-5.6 Sol","requestedReasoning":"high"},{"id":"entry-43","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","label":"GPT-5.6 Terra","requestedReasoning":"high"},{"id":"entry-58","model":"gpt-5.3-codex-spark","label":"GPT-5.3 Codex Spark","requestedReasoning":"high"},{"id":"entry-76","model":"gpt-5.5","label":"GPT-5.5","requestedReasoning":"high"}],"concepts":[{"id":"sports","category":"sports","title":"Rival fans. Same total.","body":"An over/under bet is on both teams' combined score. A 24–21 final makes 45, so over 44.5 wins. Your team's result is a separate story.","takeaway":"For totals, add both scores. Then compare with the line.","rationale":"Two people can cheer for different teams and still be watching the same betting total. The hook uses that social contrast to introduce one precise distinction: an over/under bet concerns the combined score. The final-score example makes the distinction usable without odds jargon or a claim about which bet to place.","caption":"Your team has a rival. The total has two contributors. An over/under bet uses both teams' combined score: a 24–21 final makes 45, so over 44.5 wins. For totals, add the scores before comparing with the line. Share this with your favorite rival fan.","photoPrompt":"Create one original candid editorial photograph, portrait 3:4 at 2K. Two friends, both visibly in their forties, sit together in outdoor football stadium seats during a quiet break in the action. A South Asian man wears a plain rust-orange scarf over a navy jacket; a white woman wears a plain emerald scarf over a cream jacket. Their different fan colors suggest friendly rivalry without representing any real team. Capture the man turning toward his friend mid-conversation, eyebrows lifted, while she answers with an amused half-smile and a small open-hand gesture. Their shoulders lean comfortably toward each other. This is a conversation between fans, not a victory celebration. Frame both from the waist up at their seated eye level with their faces and complete gesturing hands inside the central 80 percent of the portrait. Keep at least 10 percent breathing room around faces and hands. Soft rows of stadium seating establish the setting; no other people appear. Late-afternoon directional sunlight lights both faces warmly, with natural pores and skin color, gentle amber highlights, navy-tinted shadows and restrained editorial contrast. Both faces should remain sharp, the seats softly blurred. No posed camera smiles. No minors, alcohol, drinks, cash, betting slips, gambling equipment, logos, brands, watermarks, signs, numbers, lettering, screens or any text in the photograph. All typography will be added separately outside the image.","photoAlt":"Two adult friends in contrasting orange and green scarves share an amused conversation in football stadium seats, lit by warm afternoon sun.","photoRationale":"The contrasting scarves give the rivalry a human setting, while their easy conversation supplies the shared experience behind 'same total.' A pause in the action avoids implying that either person has won a bet. No scoreboard or generated number is needed to prove the arithmetic.","sources":[{"path":"how-to-play/sports-betting.md","section":"Totals (Over/Under)","note":"Defines a total as the combined score of both teams, independent of which team wins. The invented final score is 24–21: 24 + 21 = 45, and 45 > 44.5. The example uses the final score counted by the market; it makes no claim about an actual event, payout, odds or special settlement rule."},{"path":"visual-identity/photography/photography.md","section":"Sports / Entertainment","note":"Supports a focus on adult fans, stadium context, warm light and social interaction rather than athletes or game equipment. The brief raises the minimum depicted age to 25."}],"tags":{"content_type":"quick-reference","pillar":["open","social"],"tier":1,"tone":"playful-witty","audience":["sports-bettors","general"],"channel":["social-media"],"reading_level":"grade-6-8"},"alt":"Intended navy Playbook social card with the program wordmark and category 'Sports betting'. Headline: Rival fans. Same total. A portrait photograph on the left shows: Two adult friends in contrasting orange and green scarves share an amused conversation in football stadium seats, lit by warm afternoon sun. Body on the right: An over/under bet is on both teams' combined score. A 24–21 final makes 45, so over 44.5 wins. Your team's result is a separate story. Green takeaway below: For totals, add both scores. Then compare with the line. A protected footer contains the template-supplied support number and age disclaimer. The photograph is an authored intention pending review of the generated image.","participant":"entry-12","png":"rendered/entry-12/sports.png","accessibleAlt":"Rival fans. Same total. An over/under bet is on both teams' combined score. A 24–21 final makes 45, so over 44.5 wins. Your team's result is a separate story. For totals, add both scores. Then compare with the line. Two adults in contrasting orange and green scarves talk in stadium seats, gesturing with open hands in warm light.","observations":[],"issues":[],"photoStatus":"generated","photoModel":"gemini-3-pro-image","photoElapsedMs":22989,"photoRecord":"photography-output/photo-round-02-20260904/entry-12/shared-input/sports/attempt-1/result.json","manifestPath":"first-submissions/entry-12/manifest.json","freezeRecord":"first-submissions/entry-12/record.json"},{"id":"myth","category":"myth","title":"Sentimental value. Same odds.","body":"Your birthday can make a roulette number feel special. On a fair European wheel with one zero, each number still has a 1-in-37 chance on every spin.","takeaway":"A meaningful number isn't a more likely number.","rationale":"This debunks the lucky-number idea without dismissing why people care about their numbers. 'Sentimental value' acknowledges the real personal connection; 'same odds' supplies the correction. Naming a fair, single-zero European wheel keeps the 1-in-37 claim specific and complete on the card.","caption":"A number can mean a lot to you. That meaning doesn't change its chance on a roulette wheel. On a fair European wheel with one zero, every number has a 1-in-37 chance on every spin. Birthdays make good memories; they don't make one number more likely. A fact for the birthday group chat.","photoPrompt":"Create one original candid editorial photograph, portrait 3:4 at 2K. A small birthday moment between two friends at a rooftop dinner table just before sunset. A Black man in his late forties, wearing a plain navy knit shirt, looks up with a surprised, delighted smile at an East Asian woman in her early fifties who has just set a small birthday cake on the table between them. She meets his eyes with a knowing smile, her hands relaxed on the tabletop beside the plate. The cake has one simple unnumbered lit candle and plain cream icing with no writing. The emotional focal point is their eye contact and shared memory, not the cake. Compose a medium seated portrait with both faces, the cake and both pairs of hands fully visible within the central 80 percent of the frame, leaving at least 10 percent breathing room around faces and hands. A softly blurred rooftop railing and evening sky give a little environmental context. Warm golden side light and a discreet warm practical light keep both faces well exposed; natural skin texture, subtle age lines, rich navy shadows and gentle amber highlights. Use moderate depth of field so both friends are sharp. Authentic off-camera expressions, no staged toast or advertising pose. Only these two clearly adult people appear. No minors, alcohol, drinks, cash, gambling equipment, phones, screens, brands, logos, watermarks, decorations with letters or numbers, or any text anywhere in the photograph. This photograph will sit beside copy, with no typography over the image.","photoAlt":"At a rooftop table in warm sunset light, two adult friends exchange delighted smiles over a small birthday cake with one plain candle.","photoRationale":"The birthday scene shows what a personal number really represents: a memory and a connection with someone else. That lets the card correct a probability claim without making the sentimental attachment the butt of the joke. The photo provides emotional context; the copy alone establishes the roulette variant and odds.","sources":[{"path":"messaging/myth-busting.md","section":"Myth 10: Choosing \"Your\" Numbers Improves Odds","note":"Explains that personal number selection does not change roulette probability and gives European roulette as 1 in 37. The visible copy explicitly assumes a fair single-zero European wheel with 37 equally likely pockets; it does not extend this denominator to double-zero or other variants."},{"path":"brand-book/02-brand-personality.md","section":"Witty / Not Preachy","note":"Directs humor toward misconceptions and keeps respect for the reader. The concept acknowledges the birthday connection while correcting only the odds claim."},{"path":"visual-identity/photography/photography.md","section":"Social / Group","note":"Supports small groups, rooftop settings, relaxed candid interaction and shared experiences. The birthday is illustrative human context, not evidence for a statistical claim."}],"tags":{"content_type":"myth-bust","pillar":["open","social"],"tier":1,"tone":"playful-witty","audience":["general","traditional-players"],"channel":["social-media"],"reading_level":"grade-6-8"},"alt":"Intended navy Playbook social card with the program wordmark and category 'Myth check'. Headline: Sentimental value. Same odds. A portrait photograph on the left shows: At a rooftop table in warm sunset light, two adult friends exchange delighted smiles over a small birthday cake with one plain candle. Body on the right: Your birthday can make a roulette number feel special. On a fair European wheel with one zero, each number still has a 1-in-37 chance on every spin. Green takeaway below: A meaningful number isn't a more likely number. A protected footer contains the template-supplied support number and age disclaimer. The photograph is an authored intention pending review of the generated image.","participant":"entry-12","png":"rendered/entry-12/myth.png","accessibleAlt":"Sentimental value. Same odds. Your birthday can make a roulette number feel special. On a fair European wheel with one zero, each number still has a 1-in-37 chance on every spin. A meaningful number isn't a more likely number. Two adults smile at each other beside a small birthday cake with one candle on a rooftop table at sunset.","observations":[],"issues":[],"photoStatus":"generated","photoModel":"gemini-3-pro-image","photoElapsedMs":25017,"photoRecord":"photography-output/photo-round-02-20260904/entry-12/shared-input/myth/attempt-1/result.json","manifestPath":"first-submissions/entry-12/manifest.json","freezeRecord":"first-submissions/entry-12/record.json"},{"id":"wildcard","category":"wildcard","title":"The penny isn't the price.","body":"A slot with 20 lines at 5 credits per line and 1¢ per credit costs $1 per spin, before any extras. The penny is the credit value.","takeaway":"Check the total bet per spin before you press spin.","rationale":"The small denomination on a slot can overshadow the full price of a spin. This concept turns that mismatch into a concise hook, then shows how lines, credits and coin value combine. It is a price-literacy topic distinct from sports totals and lucky-number probability, with a specific check the reader can make.","caption":"A penny names the credit value. The line bet has more math: 20 lines × 5 credits per line × 1¢ per credit = $1 per spin, before any extras. Those are example settings, not a price for every slot. Check the total bet per spin before you press spin. Save this for a clearer read of the controls.","photoPrompt":"Create one original candid editorial photograph, portrait 3:4 at 2K. Two adult friends pause together at one slot machine to understand its controls before starting. A Latina woman in her late thirties sits upright in the machine's chair, wearing a plain emerald blouse. A Black woman in her mid-fifties, in a plain navy jacket, sits on an adjacent chair turned toward her. They have relaxed, interested expressions: the first has just glanced back from the machine toward her friend, and the friend responds with a small open-palm gesture. The moment should feel like two capable adults comparing notes, not a lesson or a win celebration. Photograph from beside the machine, facing the women, so only a narrow unmarked side edge of the cabinet is visible at one edge of the frame. Its screen, reels and control panel are entirely outside the view. No rows of gaming equipment or decorative chips. Their interaction is the clear subject, with their complete hands resting near the chair arms and their faces sharp. Frame both from the waist up within the central 80 percent of the portrait and preserve at least 10 percent breathing room around faces and hands. Soft architectural bokeh suggests a modern entertainment lounge. A clear warm overhead practical and soft side fill light both faces naturally; preserve pores, age lines and natural skin color, with rich navy shadows, warm amber highlights and no cold neon cast. Only these two clearly adult people appear. No minors, alcohol, drinks, cash, distress, brands, logos, watermarks, signs, numbers, lettering, readable displays or any text in the photograph. All card typography is added separately outside the image.","photoAlt":"Two adult women sit beside a slot machine, exchanging a relaxed, interested look while discussing its controls; only the cabinet side is visible in warm lounge light.","photoRationale":"The pause to understand the controls gives the price check an ordinary social setting. Keeping the interface outside the frame prevents invented machine text from contradicting the arithmetic, and the narrow cabinet edge earns its place by identifying the moment in which checking the total bet is useful.","sources":[{"path":"how-to-play/slots.md","section":"Playing a spin","note":"States that the total bet equals active lines multiplied by the bet per line. This example has 20 active lines and 5 credits bet per line, making 100 credits."},{"path":"how-to-play/slots.md","section":"Bet types","note":"Defines coin denomination as the base value of each credit. At $0.01 per credit, 20 × 5 × $0.01 = $1 per spin. All three example settings and the exclusion of extras are visible in the body. The calculation covers this line-based wager, excluding any extra side bet or feature purchase; it does not claim all penny slots cost $1."},{"path":"visual-identity/photography/photography.md","section":"Game & equipment imagery","note":"Permits equipment when it provides purposeful gameplay context. The small cabinet edge identifies a controls-checking moment while the people remain the subject; no generated interface establishes the numerical claim."}],"tags":{"content_type":"quick-reference","pillar":["open"],"tier":1,"tone":"confident-informative","audience":["general","traditional-players"],"channel":["social-media"],"reading_level":"grade-6-8"},"alt":"Intended navy Playbook social card with the program wordmark and category 'Your play'. Headline: The penny isn't the price. A portrait photograph on the left shows: Two adult women sit beside a slot machine, exchanging a relaxed, interested look while discussing its controls; only the cabinet side is visible in warm lounge light. Body on the right: A slot with 20 lines at 5 credits per line and 1¢ per credit costs $1 per spin, before any extras. The penny is the credit value. Green takeaway below: Check the total bet per spin before you press spin. A protected footer contains the template-supplied support number and age disclaimer. The photograph is an authored intention pending review of the generated image.","participant":"entry-12","png":"rendered/entry-12/wildcard.png","accessibleAlt":"The penny isn't the price. A slot with 20 lines at 5 credits per line and 1¢ per credit costs $1 per spin, before any extras. The penny is the credit value. Check the total bet per spin before you press spin. Two adult women talk beside a gaming-machine cabinet in a warmly lit lounge; one gestures while the other rests her hands by the controls.","observations":["The photo shows more of the machine's controls than the prompt requested. The card's arithmetic remains in the separate text panel and does not depend on this generated interface."],"issues":[],"photoStatus":"generated","photoModel":"gemini-3-pro-image","photoElapsedMs":26656,"photoRecord":"photography-output/photo-round-02-20260904/entry-12/shared-input/wildcard/attempt-1/result.json","manifestPath":"first-submissions/entry-12/manifest.json","freezeRecord":"first-submissions/entry-12/record.json"},{"id":"sports","category":"sports","title":"Half Right Doesn't Break Even","body":"At standard −110 odds, winning half your bets does not break even. You risk $110 to win $100, so the break-even rate is about 52.4%.","takeaway":"Check the break-even rate before you place the bet.","rationale":"The hook challenges the familiar idea that winning half means staying level. The body shows the exact −110 assumption and the arithmetic in plain language, while the takeaway gives bettors a reusable check for any line.","caption":"Winning half sounds level. At standard −110 odds, it isn't: risking $110 to win $100 puts break-even at about 52.4%. Check the rate before you place the bet. #KnowTheOdds","alt":"A navy Playbook social card titled “Half Right Doesn't Break Even.” The body reads: “At standard −110 odds, winning half your bets does not break even. You risk $110 to win $100, so the break-even rate is about 52.4%.” The green takeaway reads: “Check the break-even rate before you place the bet.” At left, three adult friends discuss a game at a warm home watch party while one casually checks an unreadable phone.","photoPrompt":"Candid premium editorial portrait photograph, 3:4 vertical composition, of three clearly adult friends aged 28 to 40 with varied genders and ethnicities at a relaxed home sports watch party. One person casually holds a smartphone with the screen angled away and completely unreadable while the other two lean in, talking through the game with curious, friendly expressions; emphasize shared discussion rather than victory or disappointment. A television glow and subtle sports-night atmosphere sit softly out of focus in the background, with no visible score, team marks, broadcast text, or logos. Warm cinematic window-and-lamp key light, natural skin texture, modern lived-in setting, moderate shallow depth of field, realistic documentary moment, premium lifestyle campaign quality. Compose for a centered 3:4 crop with at least 10% breathing room around every face and hand. No minors, no alcohol, no gambling equipment, no cash, no brands, no watermarks, no readable screens, no text in the image.","photoAlt":"Three adult friends at a warm home watch party discuss the game while one casually checks an unreadable phone.","photoRationale":"A shared watch-party conversation makes break-even math feel native to sports culture. The phone supplies casual betting context without asking the image to prove the number, and the group's thoughtful interaction matches Playbook's peer-to-peer voice.","sources":[{"path":"how-to-play/sports-betting.md","section":"Break-even win rates","note":"The table gives a 52.4% break-even win rate at −110. The visible assumption is $110 risked for $100 profit; 110 ÷ (110 + 100) = 52.38%, rounded to 52.4%."}],"tags":{"content_type":"quick-reference","pillar":["open"],"tier":1,"tone":"confident-informative","audience":["sports-bettors"],"channel":["social-media"],"reading_level":"grade-6-8"},"participant":"entry-27","png":"rendered/entry-27/sports.png","accessibleAlt":"Half Right Doesn't Break Even At standard −110 odds, winning half your bets does not break even. You risk $110 to win $100, so the break-even rate is about 52.4%. Check the break-even rate before you place the bet. Three adults converse on a sofa beside an unreadable phone, with a blurred television and warm living-room lighting behind them.","observations":["The 52.4% break-even rate assumes equal stakes at −110 and excludes pushes. The card states the $110/$100 price but does not explicitly state those assumptions."],"issues":[],"photoStatus":"generated","photoModel":"gemini-3-pro-image","photoElapsedMs":23431,"photoRecord":"photography-output/photo-round-02-20260904/entry-27/shared-input/sports/attempt-1/result.json","manifestPath":"first-submissions/entry-27/manifest.json","freezeRecord":"first-submissions/entry-27/record.json"},{"id":"myth","category":"myth","title":"Your Number Didn't Get Luckier","body":"On an American roulette wheel, every number has the same 1-in-38 chance on every spin. Past spins—and why you picked the number—do not change it.","takeaway":"Keep the ritual. Know the 1-in-38 math.","rationale":"The concept respects the fun of choosing a personal number while separating the ritual from probability. Naming the American-wheel assumption keeps the claim precise and turns a familiar casino moment into a shareable myth check.","caption":"Birthday, jersey number, favorite number—pick the one you like. On an American roulette wheel, each still gets the same 1-in-38 chance every spin. The ritual is yours; the math stays put. #MythCheck","alt":"A navy Playbook social card titled “Your Number Didn't Get Luckier.” The body reads: “On an American roulette wheel, every number has the same 1-in-38 chance on every spin. Past spins—and why you picked the number—do not change it.” The green takeaway reads: “Keep the ritual. Know the 1-in-38 math.” At left, three adult friends share a playful moment at a roulette table under warm cinematic light; the wheel and layout are secondary and their markings are unreadable.","photoPrompt":"Candid high-end editorial portrait photograph, 3:4 vertical composition, of three clearly adult friends aged 30 to 45 with varied genders and ethnicities sharing a playful, connected moment at a modern upscale roulette table. One friend lightly gestures toward a place on the felt while the others recognize the choice with warm smiles; focus on their faces, hands, and interaction. The roulette wheel and a small ordinary group of chips provide necessary context but remain secondary, softly focused, and never glamorous; all wheel and table numerals must be too blurred or obscured to read. Intentional warm cinematic key light, well-exposed natural skin, lifted shadows, subtle amber highlights, realistic candid body language, premium hospitality editorial quality. Compose for a centered 3:4 crop with at least 10% breathing room around faces and hands. No minors, no alcohol, no cash stacks, no solitary players, no distress, no brands, no logos, no watermarks, no readable screens, no readable symbols or text in the image.","photoAlt":"Three adult friends share a playful number-picking moment at a warmly lit roulette table with all markings unreadable.","photoRationale":"The scene makes the personal-number ritual visible through social interaction, which gives the myth emotional context without mocking it. Keeping the wheel secondary lets the card's copy carry the exact 1-in-38 claim.","sources":[{"path":"messaging/myth-busting.md","section":"Myth 10: Choosing \"Your\" Numbers Improves Odds","note":"This section states that every number on an American roulette wheel has a 1-in-38 chance on every spin and that personal choice or past spins do not alter the probability."}],"tags":{"content_type":"myth-bust","pillar":["open","social"],"tier":1,"tone":"playful-witty","audience":["general"],"channel":["social-media"],"reading_level":"grade-6-8"},"participant":"entry-27","png":"rendered/entry-27/myth.png","accessibleAlt":"Your Number Didn't Get Luckier On an American roulette wheel, every number has the same 1-in-38 chance on every spin. Past spins—and why you picked the number—do not change it. Keep the ritual. Know the 1-in-38 math. Three adults smile around a roulette table, one pointing at the felt; the wheel, chips, and generated table numerals are visible in front.","observations":["The roulette numerals are readable despite the prompt's request to blur or obscure them. Generated table and wheel details are not reliable evidence of a correct American wheel.","The 1-in-38 claim uses the conventional fair double-zero American wheel. Naming that variant explicitly would avoid ambiguity before publication."],"issues":[],"photoStatus":"generated","photoModel":"gemini-3-pro-image","photoElapsedMs":23070,"photoRecord":"photography-output/photo-round-02-20260904/entry-27/shared-input/myth/attempt-1/result.json","manifestPath":"first-submissions/entry-27/manifest.json","freezeRecord":"first-submissions/entry-27/record.json"},{"id":"wildcard","category":"wildcard","title":"Small Spins. Full-Speed Math.","body":"At $0.50 a spin every 3 seconds, you make 20 spins a minute. That is $10 wagered per minute, or $600 in an hour if the pace holds.","takeaway":"Choose your pace and budget before the first spin.","rationale":"Small denominations can hide the speed of total wagering. The card makes pace visible with simple arithmetic, then turns the insight into a concrete pre-session choice without promising an account control.","caption":"Small bets can move at full speed. At $0.50 every 3 seconds, the pace is 20 spins and $10 wagered each minute—$600 in an hour if it holds. Pick your pace and entertainment budget first. #YourPlay","alt":"A navy Playbook social card titled “Small Spins. Full-Speed Math.” The body reads: “At $0.50 a spin every 3 seconds, you make 20 spins a minute. That is $10 wagered per minute, or $600 in an hour if the pace holds.” The green takeaway reads: “Choose your pace and budget before the first spin.” At left, two adult friends pause together near a bright casino lounge; one checks a wristwatch while the other holds an unreadable phone.","photoPrompt":"Candid premium lifestyle editorial portrait photograph, 3:4 vertical composition, of two clearly adult friends aged 30 to 45 with different genders and ethnic backgrounds pausing together at the edge of a bright, modern casino entertainment lounge before playing. One person casually checks a wristwatch while the other holds a smartphone at their side with the screen completely unreadable; they face each other in an easy, relaxed conversation, suggesting a shared plan for time and budget. Any gaming area appears only as soft environmental bokeh in the deep background, never as the subject. Warm intentional cinematic light with a clear flattering key, natural skin texture, lifted navy-toned shadows, subtle amber highlights, realistic unstaged body language, premium contemporary editorial quality. Compose for a centered 3:4 crop with at least 10% breathing room around faces, hands, watch, and phone. No minors, no alcohol, no chips, no cash, no visible wagers, no solitary or distressed players, no brands, no logos, no watermarks, no readable screens, no text in the image.","photoAlt":"Two adult friends pause in a warm casino lounge, one checking a wristwatch while the other holds an unreadable phone.","photoRationale":"The watch and shared pause translate wagering speed into an ordinary planning moment. The softly contextual venue keeps the message tied to play while the relaxed conversation shows agency and social connection.","sources":[{"path":"how-to-play/slots.md","section":"Tips for informed play","note":"Tip 4 gives the same $0.50-per-spin, one-spin-every-3-seconds example and states that it totals $600 per hour. Arithmetic check: 60 ÷ 3 = 20 spins per minute; 20 × $0.50 = $10 per minute; $10 × 60 = $600 per hour."}],"tags":{"content_type":"quick-reference","pillar":["open"],"tier":1,"tone":"confident-informative","audience":["general"],"channel":["social-media"],"reading_level":"grade-6-8"},"participant":"entry-27","png":"rendered/entry-27/wildcard.png","accessibleAlt":"Small Spins. Full-Speed Math. At $0.50 a spin every 3 seconds, you make 20 spins a minute. That is $10 wagered per minute, or $600 in an hour if the pace holds. Choose your pace and budget before the first spin. Two adults talk in a casino lounge, one looking at a wristwatch while the other holds a phone with a blank screen.","observations":["The $600 calculation is total wagering at the stated continuous pace, not $600 of net loss or required starting funds. The card correctly says 'wagered'; an explicit distinction could improve comprehension."],"issues":[],"photoStatus":"generated","photoModel":"gemini-3-pro-image","photoElapsedMs":28658,"photoRecord":"photography-output/photo-round-02-20260904/entry-27/shared-input/wildcard/attempt-1/result.json","manifestPath":"first-submissions/entry-27/manifest.json","freezeRecord":"first-submissions/entry-27/record.json"},{"id":"sports","category":"sports","title":"A Half Point Ends the Argument","body":"At -6.5, the favorite needs a seven-point win. At +6.5, the underdog can lose by six or win. The half point means neither side can tie.","takeaway":"The .5 is there so the spread has a clear winner.","rationale":"This makes the point spread feel like the game's second score: a simple rule that changes what a close finish means. The half point is the clean, shareable proof.","caption":"A point spread turns a game into a margin question. At plus or minus 6.5, the half point removes the tie. That is the whole plot twist. #KnowTheOdds","alt":"Intended Playbook RG sports-betting card titled 'A Half Point Ends the Argument.' It explains that a -6.5 favorite needs a seven-point win, while a +6.5 underdog can lose by six or win, beside a photo of friends discussing a close basketball game.","photoPrompt":"Candid editorial lifestyle photograph for an entertainment-literacy social card, portrait 3:4 composition at 2K. Three clearly adult friends aged about 28 to 40, diverse in appearance, sit together in a modern arena lounge during a basketball game; one uses both hands to illustrate a narrow scoring margin while the others lean in, smiling and talking. Show social connection and an informed, lively conversation, not a bet being placed. Warm cinematic key light with amber highlights, subtle navy-toned shadows, natural skin tones, moderate shallow depth of field, premium magazine editorial realism. Compose as a centered medium portrait with comfortable breathing room around every face and hand. Background crowd and court lights are softly out of focus with no readable scoreboard, screen content, signage, logos, brands, watermarks, text, cash, betting slips, gambling equipment, alcohol, minors, distress, or solitary subjects.","photoAlt":"Three adult friends in an arena lounge talk animatedly during a basketball game, with one gesturing a small scoring margin in warm cinematic light.","photoRationale":"A shared close-game conversation gives the point spread a human sports context while the hand gesture suggests a narrow margin without pretending the photo proves the rule.","sources":[{"path":"how-to-play/sports-betting.md","section":"Point spread","note":"Defines the favorite as needing to win by more than the spread and the underdog as able to lose by less or win outright. The source's -6.5/+6.5 examples establish the seven-point and six-point arithmetic; the half point eliminates ties."}],"tags":{"content_type":"quick-reference","pillar":["open","social"],"tier":1,"tone":"confident-informative","audience":["general","sports-bettors"],"channel":["social-media"],"reading_level":"grade-6-8"},"participant":"entry-43","png":"rendered/entry-43/sports.png","accessibleAlt":"A Half Point Ends the Argument At -6.5, the favorite needs a seven-point win. At +6.5, the underdog can lose by six or win. The half point means neither side can tie. The .5 is there so the spread has a clear winner. Three adults sit at a table overlooking a basketball arena; the middle person makes a small-distance hand gesture while the others listen.","observations":["The favorite needs a win by at least seven, and the underdog can lose by six or fewer. The current wording names boundary examples but could be read as exact margins. 'Neither side can tie' should clearly refer to a settled spread bet, not the game's score."],"issues":[],"photoStatus":"generated","photoModel":"gemini-3-pro-image","photoElapsedMs":23968,"photoRecord":"photography-output/photo-round-02-20260904/entry-43/shared-input/sports/attempt-1/result.json","manifestPath":"first-submissions/entry-43/manifest.json","freezeRecord":"first-submissions/entry-43/record.json"},{"id":"myth","category":"myth","title":"The Slot Keeps No Tab","body":"Every spin gets a new, independent result. Past losses do not load the next spin, and past wins do not make the next one less likely.","takeaway":"The next spin starts fresh. The machine does not keep score.","rationale":"The tab metaphor corrects the 'due' myth without scolding. It gives players one memorable way to understand independent outcomes.","caption":"A slot does not keep a running tab of wins or losses. Each spin is a fresh result. The math is not holding a grudge. #KnowTheOdds","alt":"Intended Playbook RG myth-check card titled 'The Slot Keeps No Tab.' It says every spin gets a new independent result and shows two friends talking beside an out-of-focus casino display.","photoPrompt":"Candid editorial lifestyle photograph for an entertainment-literacy myth-check card, portrait 3:4 composition at 2K. Two clearly adult friends aged about 30 to 45, diverse in appearance, stand together in an upscale casino lounge and share a knowing conversational smile; one has an open, relaxed hand near the edge of a generic, heavily out-of-focus slot display while the other responds with an expressive gesture. The people and their conversation are the focus, not the machine. Warm cinematic directional key light, amber highlights, soft navy shadows, natural skin tones, subtle contrast, premium magazine editorial realism, moderate shallow depth of field. Center the pair in a medium portrait with breathing room around faces and hands. The display must show no readable symbols, numbers, screen content, text, brands, logos, watermarks, cash, chips, betting slips, alcohol, minors, distress, or solitary subjects.","photoAlt":"Two adult friends share a conversational smile in a warm casino lounge, beside a blurred generic display with no readable content.","photoRationale":"The social, relaxed exchange reframes a slot myth as a fact worth discussing. The blurred display supplies only relevant game context; it does not stand in for the mathematical claim.","sources":[{"path":"how-to-play/slots.md","section":"Tips for informed play","note":"States that every spin is independent and that the last spin, or last 1,000 spins, has zero effect on the next one. No numerical assumption is used."},{"path":"messaging/myth-busting.md","section":"Myth 2: Due for a Win","note":"Supports the correction that a machine has no memory and past losses do not affect the next independent outcome."}],"tags":{"content_type":"myth-bust","pillar":["open","social"],"tier":1,"tone":"playful-witty","audience":["general","traditional-players"],"channel":["social-media"],"reading_level":"grade-6-8"},"participant":"entry-43","png":"rendered/entry-43/myth.png","accessibleAlt":"The Slot Keeps No Tab Every spin gets a new, independent result. Past losses do not load the next spin, and past wins do not make the next one less likely. The next spin starts fresh. The machine does not keep score. Two adults smile beside a blurred slot machine in a warm lounge. A small foreground book displays generated myth-check lettering.","observations":["Google added a prominently readable book labelled 'Myth Check: Casino Lore' even though the prompt prohibited text. It is an image-generation departure, retained without regeneration.","'The machine does not keep score' and the caption's 'does not keep a running tab' can sound like claims about recordkeeping. Independent outcomes do not mean that gaming systems record no activity; the intended lesson is that previous independent results do not make a win due."],"issues":[],"photoStatus":"generated","photoModel":"gemini-3-pro-image","photoElapsedMs":24193,"photoRecord":"photography-output/photo-round-02-20260904/entry-43/shared-input/myth/attempt-1/result.json","manifestPath":"first-submissions/entry-43/manifest.json","freezeRecord":"first-submissions/entry-43/record.json"},{"id":"wildcard","category":"wildcard","title":"The Total Is in the Tempo","body":"$0.50 a spin, once every 3 seconds, is $10 a minute. Keep that pace for an hour and you have wagered $600—even on a penny machine.","takeaway":"Check your pace: small bets can add up quickly.","rationale":"This card puts the total in plain sight. Showing the minute-by-minute arithmetic turns a small per-spin amount into a pace players can recognize.","caption":"At 20 spins a minute, a 50-cent spin is $10 a minute. Pace is part of the game math. #KnowTheOdds","alt":"Intended Playbook RG card titled 'The Total Is in the Tempo.' It calculates that 50 cents per spin every three seconds equals $10 a minute and $600 wagered in an hour, with a photo of friends checking the time together.","photoPrompt":"Candid editorial lifestyle photograph for an entertainment-literacy social card, portrait 3:4 composition at 2K. Three clearly adult friends aged about 28 to 45, diverse in appearance, leave a stylish sports-and-entertainment lounge together at warm early evening; one casually checks an analog wristwatch while the group keeps talking and laughing. The moment should suggest awareness of time and pace within an enjoyable shared night out, never a warning scene. Warm cinematic golden-hour key light, subtle amber highlights, navy-tinted shadows, natural skin tones, medium shallow depth of field, premium magazine editorial realism. Frame a centered medium portrait with generous breathing room around faces and hands. No readable phone or watch text, screens, signage, logos, brands, watermarks, cash, chips, betting slips, gambling equipment, alcohol, minors, distress, or solitary subjects.","photoAlt":"Three adult friends leave a warm entertainment lounge together, with one casually checking an analog wristwatch while they talk and laugh.","photoRationale":"The shared time check makes pace visible in a social setting, connecting the per-spin arithmetic to a real evening without showing a wager or using the image as numerical evidence.","sources":[{"path":"how-to-play/slots.md","section":"Tips for informed play","note":"Uses the same stated pace: a $0.50 bet every 3 seconds adds to $600 per hour. Arithmetic shown on card: 60/3 = 20 spins per minute; 20 × $0.50 = $10 per minute; $10 × 60 = $600 per hour."},{"path":"how-to-play/slots.md","section":"Snippet 3","note":"Provides the supported social-card example: $0.50 per spin every 3 seconds equals about $600 per hour, including on a penny machine."}],"tags":{"content_type":"quick-reference","pillar":["open"],"tier":1,"tone":"confident-informative","audience":["general","traditional-players"],"channel":["social-media"],"reading_level":"grade-6-8"},"participant":"entry-43","png":"rendered/entry-43/wildcard.png","accessibleAlt":"The Total Is in the Tempo $0.50 a spin, once every 3 seconds, is $10 a minute. Keep that pace for an hour and you have wagered $600—even on a penny machine. Check your pace: small bets can add up quickly. Three adults walk out of an entertainment lounge in evening light, smiling while the middle person checks a wristwatch.","observations":["The $600 calculation is total wagering at the stated continuous pace, not $600 of net loss or required starting funds. The card correctly says 'wagered'; an explicit distinction could improve comprehension."],"issues":[],"photoStatus":"generated","photoModel":"gemini-3-pro-image","photoElapsedMs":25882,"photoRecord":"photography-output/photo-round-02-20260904/entry-43/shared-input/wildcard/attempt-1/result.json","manifestPath":"first-submissions/entry-43/manifest.json","freezeRecord":"first-submissions/entry-43/record.json"},{"id":"sports","category":"sports","title":"Why -110 Means a Thin Margin","body":"At standard -110 lines, both sides imply about 104.8% total probability, so the sportsbook keeps a 4.8% margin before you can beat it.","takeaway":"Break-even is 52.4% win rate. If you rely on emotion, the math wins.","rationale":"This concept keeps the point concrete by tying sports learning directly to a familiar number, then turning it into a practical check: what looked like confidence must be tested against the vig before a pick can stay profitable.","caption":"Most sports conversations start with team talk. This starts with line math: can you beat 52.4% at -110?","alt":"Top-left headline card with title 'Why -110 Means a Thin Margin' and body copy explaining both sides at -110 imply 104.8% combined and a 4.8% margin, with a green takeaway stating the break-even win rate. A candid social group photo sits left of the copy, one adult smiling and one showing a casual watch-party conversation.","photoPrompt":"Create a candid editorial 3:4 photo for social media, 2K. Indoor sports bar at warm golden-hour style lighting. Four diverse adults age 25+ sit together on a couch at a game night, laughing and leaning in together while one person points at a sports schedule on a wall TV in the background. No readable on-screen gambling or betting content, no cards, chips, cash, money stacks, logos, or text in the photograph. Natural skin tones, cinematic cinematic lighting, shallow depth of field, moderate motion, social connection visible. Leave breathing room around faces and hands for layout text. No minors, no alcohol, no gambling paraphernalia.","photoAlt":"Adults in their late twenties to forties at a sports watch night, standing close and speaking with each other while enjoying a shared atmosphere; faces are clearly lit, natural skin tones, candid celebration energy, no explicit gambling actions.","photoRationale":"The social, non-transactional interaction mirrors a real conversation about odds and keeps the concept grounded in player decision-making rather than product promotion.","sources":[{"path":"how-to-play/sports-betting.md","section":"The 30-second version","note":"Source states that standard -110 requires a 52.4% win rate to break even and that sports betting lines include a built-in vig. Assumption: this concept uses those values to derive combined implied margin arithmetic across both sides."},{"path":"how-to-play/sports-betting.md","section":"The vig in practice","note":"Source gives both-side implied probability at -110 as 52.4% each, total 104.8%, so the overage is 4.8% in this table."}],"tags":{"content_type":"quick-reference","pillar":["open","social"],"tier":1,"tone":"confident-informative","audience":["general","sports-bettors"],"channel":["social-media"],"reading_level":"grade-6-8"},"participant":"entry-58","png":"rendered/entry-58/sports.png","accessibleAlt":"Why -110 Means a Thin Margin At standard -110 lines, both sides imply about 104.8% total probability, so the sportsbook keeps a 4.8% margin before you can beat it. Break-even is 52.4% win rate. If you rely on emotion, the math wins. Four adults talk and laugh on a sofa under a sports-style display with generated lettering and numbers; clear drinking glasses sit on the table.","observations":["The core explanation conflates overround with the share of stakes retained. Two −110 sides imply about 104.76%, or a 4.76 percentage-point overround. With $110 staked on each side, $220 comes in and $210 is paid out, leaving $10/$220 ≈ 4.55%. The source table lists approximately 4.5% vig, not 4.8% kept.","The 52.4% break-even rate assumes equal stakes at −110 and excludes pushes. These assumptions are not explicit in the card.","The 'beat it' framing and 'If you rely on emotion, the math wins' warrant voice review: they can turn literacy into a profitability challenge or sound judgmental.","The photograph contains readable sports-style display text despite the no-text direction. The authored photoAlt says the group is standing, but the generated group is seated."],"issues":[],"photoStatus":"generated","photoModel":"gemini-3-pro-image","photoElapsedMs":23866,"photoRecord":"photography-output/photo-round-02-20260904/entry-58/shared-input/sports/attempt-1/result.json","manifestPath":"first-submissions/entry-58/manifest.json","freezeRecord":"first-submissions/entry-58/record.json"},{"id":"myth","category":"myth","title":"A Machine Can't Be Due","body":"A slot machine does not track your history. A no-payout streak means only that you had a bad run, not that spin 201 is due to hit — each spin is independent and decided separately.","takeaway":"History affects only your memory. The odds on the next spin stay the same.","rationale":"This myth card converts a common emotional belief into a simple sequence: lucky and unlucky runs are separate. Keeping the wording close to player language helps the card land quickly in social feeds.","caption":"If your machine hasn't paid out in 50 spins, that doesn't increase your next chance. That's how the brain reads patterns, not the RNG.","alt":"Card headline and copy debunking the \"machine is due\" myth with a green takeaway, supported by a warm candid photo of adults sharing conversation in a lounge, social context focused on people and faces rather than any gambling device.","photoPrompt":"Create a candid editorial 3:4 photo for social media, 2K. Cozy cafe lounge scene with three diverse adults aged 25+ in a warm social moment, one person holding a coaster and another gesturing while others laugh. Keep the shot human-centered with natural skin tones and soft cinematic light from late-evening window. No gambling symbols, no machines, no cards, no dice, no chips, no cash stacks, no minors, no readable screen content, no logos, no banners. Maintain breathing room around faces and hands for overlaid text.","photoAlt":"Three adults in a café booth sharing conversation and mild laughter in a warm-lit candid frame, with no emphasis on devices or gambling objects; all attention on social interaction and facial expression.","photoRationale":"The scene emphasizes myth correction through peer conversation and shared sensemaking, matching the educational tone and avoiding gambling-object imagery.","sources":[{"path":"messaging/myth-busting.md","section":"Myth 2: Due for a Win","note":"Myth buster explicitly says a machine that hasn't paid out in 200 spins has identical odds on the next spin; uses exact independent-spin framing. Assumption: claims generalize to a simple next-spin explanation."},{"path":"messaging/stigma-free-language.md","section":"Quick reference card","note":"Used to enforce tone: use neutral language around behavior and avoid alarmist labels in player-facing copy."}],"tags":{"content_type":"myth-bust","pillar":["open"],"tier":1,"tone":"playful-witty","audience":["general"],"channel":["social-media"],"reading_level":"grade-6-8"},"participant":"entry-58","png":"rendered/entry-58/myth.png","accessibleAlt":"A Machine Can't Be Due A slot machine does not track your history. A no-payout streak means only that you had a bad run, not that spin 201 is due to hit — each spin is independent and decided separately. History affects only your memory. The odds on the next spin stay the same. Three adults laugh together in a warm café seating area, with one holding a dark coaster and mugs on the table.","observations":["'A slot machine does not track your history' is broader than the supported claim about independent outcomes. Gaming systems can record activity; the intended lesson is that a losing streak does not make the next independent spin due.","The photograph shows a general café conversation. Whether it adds enough context to this particular slot myth is an open creative-review question."],"issues":[],"photoStatus":"generated","photoModel":"gemini-3-pro-image","photoElapsedMs":25549,"photoRecord":"photography-output/photo-round-02-20260904/entry-58/shared-input/myth/attempt-1/result.json","manifestPath":"first-submissions/entry-58/manifest.json","freezeRecord":"first-submissions/entry-58/record.json"},{"id":"wildcard","category":"wildcard","title":"Your Long-Run Cost, Not a Promise","body":"A 95% RTP slot has a 5% house edge. Over time, that is about $5 lost for every $100 you wager, even though any single session can move either way.","takeaway":"Set your session budget first. The edge is math, not hope.","rationale":"This keeps the card practical: one headline fact and one behavior-ready takeaway. It teaches expected value without sounding like fear messaging and gives a concrete action in the same glance.","caption":"There's no lucky bankroll plan for long-run math. A 95% RTP machine means house edge first, hope second.","alt":"Playbook card showing the long-run expectation concept with headline and body about RTP and house edge, paired with a candid photograph of adults discussing budgeting and entertainment plans in a home game space.","photoPrompt":"Create a candid editorial 3:4 photo for social media, 2K. Indoor home setting with two adults and one friend, all over 25, sitting at a table reviewing a printed notepad and calculator while drinking coffee. They appear thoughtful and social, no one gambling at a machine. No visible logos, no readable text on any device, no cash stacks, no betting slips, no alcohol, no minors. Warm cinematic key light, natural skin tones, moderate depth of field, social connection with enough negative space for overlay text.","photoAlt":"Three adults (25+) in a home living room at a table discussing plans, with relaxed postures and clear eye contact, emphasizing budgeting conversation rather than wagering behavior.","photoRationale":"The photo supports the math claim by showing intentional planning and shared decision-making, reinforcing that risk management is a habit, not a vibe.","sources":[{"path":"how-to-play/slots.md","section":"What this means for your wallet","note":"Source gives a direct translation of 95% RTP into 5% house edge and $5 expected loss per $100 over time. Assumption: used as a direct long-run expectation statement with normal variance caveat."},{"path":"how-to-play/slots.md","section":"House edge and RTP","note":"Source defines house edge as what the casino keeps, reinforcing the same expected-value relationship."}],"tags":{"content_type":"quick-reference","pillar":["open","social"],"tier":1,"tone":"confident-informative","audience":["general"],"channel":["social-media"],"reading_level":"grade-6-8"},"participant":"entry-58","png":"rendered/entry-58/wildcard.png","accessibleAlt":"Your Long-Run Cost, Not a Promise A 95% RTP slot has a 5% house edge. Over time, that is about $5 lost for every $100 you wager, even though any single session can move either way. Set your session budget first. The edge is math, not hope. Three adults lean over a notebook and calculator at a kitchen table, with coffee mugs and generated handwriting visible.","observations":["The long-run $5-per-$100 relationship is an expectation, not a guaranteed personal outcome. 'On average' would make that distinction more explicit; the existing title and session caveat help.","The caption's 'house edge first, hope second' and the takeaway's 'math, not hope' warrant a tone review against the brand's informed-choice voice.","Generated writing and calculator numerals appear in the photo. The prompt requested a notepad and calculator without explicitly prohibiting readable paper text, although the shared brief prohibited text in the photograph."],"issues":[],"photoStatus":"generated","photoModel":"gemini-3-pro-image","photoElapsedMs":22886,"photoRecord":"photography-output/photo-round-02-20260904/entry-58/shared-input/wildcard/attempt-1/result.json","manifestPath":"first-submissions/entry-58/manifest.json","freezeRecord":"first-submissions/entry-58/record.json"},{"id":"sports","category":"sports","title":"Half Right Still Pays the Book","body":"At -110, winning half your spread bets is still short. You need about 52.4% just to break even because the vig is baked into the line.","takeaway":"Check the price before the pick.","rationale":"This turns a familiar confidence trap into one clean number. A player can understand the sportsbook's margin before treating a 50/50 opinion as enough.","caption":"Half right sounds fine until the vig shows up. At standard -110 odds, you need about 52.4% just to break even. Check the price before the pick.","alt":"A Playbook social card for sports betting titled Half Right Still Pays the Book. The body explains that at -110, winning half your spread bets is still short and you need about 52.4% to break even because the vig is baked into the line. The left photo shows adult friends watching sports together. The takeaway says Check the price before the pick.","photoPrompt":"Candid editorial portrait photograph, 3:4 aspect ratio, 2K. Three clearly adult friends aged 30 to 45 at a warm evening sports watch party in a modern living room, gathered near a sofa with a large out-of-focus game on TV in the background. One person casually holds a phone angled away with only soft screen glow and no readable content, while the others lean in, smiling and discussing the game. Warm cinematic practical lighting, natural skin tones, diverse adults, relaxed social connection, no alcohol, no minors, no logos, no team brands, no readable text, no betting slips, no cash, no watermark. Compose with faces and hands comfortably inside frame and breathing room for a centered 3:4 crop.","photoAlt":"Three adult friends watching a game together in warm living-room light while one casually holds a phone with no readable screen content.","photoRationale":"The watch-party scene grounds sports betting in social entertainment while the turned-away phone suggests checking the line without asking the image to prove the 52.4% claim.","sources":[{"path":"how-to-play/sports-betting.md","section":"The 30-second version","note":"Supports the claim that standard -110 odds require a 52.4% win rate to break even and that the sportsbook's line accounts for available information."},{"path":"how-to-play/sports-betting.md","section":"Where the sportsbook makes money","note":"Explains vig as the sportsbook's built-in commission and states that -110 means betting $110 to win $100."},{"path":"how-to-play/sports-betting.md","section":"Break-even win rates","note":"Lists -110 at a 52.4% break-even rate. Arithmetic: 110 / (110 + 100) = 52.38%, rounded to 52.4%."}],"tags":{"content_type":"quick-reference","pillar":["open"],"tier":1,"tone":"confident-informative","audience":["sports-bettors","general"],"channel":["social-media"],"reading_level":"grade-6-8"},"participant":"entry-76","png":"rendered/entry-76/sports.png","accessibleAlt":"Half Right Still Pays the Book At -110, winning half your spread bets is still short. You need about 52.4% just to break even because the vig is baked into the line. Check the price before the pick. Three adults talk behind a sofa in a warm living room, with one holding a phone and a sports broadcast visible on the television.","observations":["The 52.4% break-even rate assumes equal stakes at −110 and excludes pushes. Those assumptions are not explicit on the card.","Broadcast-style graphics remain visible on the television. The original prompt requested no readable text; check the full-resolution image before publication."],"issues":[],"photoStatus":"generated","photoModel":"gemini-3-pro-image","photoElapsedMs":23771,"photoRecord":"photography-output/photo-round-02-20260904/entry-76/shared-input/sports/attempt-1/result.json","manifestPath":"first-submissions/entry-76/manifest.json","freezeRecord":"first-submissions/entry-76/record.json"},{"id":"myth","category":"myth","title":"Almost Is Just Theater","body":"A slot near miss feels close, but the RNG already chose the result before the reels moved. The next spin starts fresh; the last display has no vote.","takeaway":"Near misses do not warm up the next spin.","rationale":"The hook respects the feeling of a near miss while naming what actually happened. It gives the player a useful rule without mocking the ritual or the reaction.","caption":"Two symbols and a blank can feel like the game winked at you. It did not. The RNG already picked the result, and the next spin starts fresh.","alt":"A Playbook myth-check card titled Almost Is Just Theater. The body explains that a slot near miss feels close, but the RNG already chose the result before the reels moved, and the next spin starts fresh. The left photo shows adult friends in a warm casino lounge with blurred slot lights behind them. The takeaway says Near misses do not warm up the next spin.","photoPrompt":"Candid editorial portrait photograph, 3:4 aspect ratio, 2K. Four clearly adult friends aged 30 or older in an upscale casino lounge, standing together in warm cinematic light with softly blurred slot-machine colors far in the background. The people are the focus: relaxed expressions, one person mid-laugh, another gesturing lightly with open hands as if reacting to a story. Natural skin tones, diverse adults, social energy, no minors, no alcohol, no cash stacks, no chips, no readable screen content, no readable signs, no brands, no watermark. Keep the gambling equipment abstract and out of focus, with breathing room around faces and hands for a centered portrait crop.","photoAlt":"Four adult friends laughing together in a warmly lit casino lounge with abstract, blurred slot-machine colors in the background.","photoRationale":"The social casino lounge gives the slot myth relevant context while keeping attention on people, not machines. The blurred background avoids relying on the photo for the RNG or near-miss explanation.","sources":[{"path":"messaging/myth-busting.md","section":"Myth 4: Near Misses Mean You're Close","note":"Supports the claim that a near miss is not almost winning and that the result was decided when the player pressed spin."},{"path":"how-to-play/slots.md","section":"Playing a spin","note":"Explains that the RNG generates the result, maps it to symbols, and the reels display an outcome already decided before animation."},{"path":"how-to-play/slots.md","section":"Tips for informed play","note":"Supports the takeaway that every spin is independent and past spins have zero effect on the next one."}],"tags":{"content_type":"myth-bust","pillar":["open","social"],"tier":1,"tone":"playful-witty","audience":["general"],"channel":["social-media"],"reading_level":"grade-6-8"},"participant":"entry-76","png":"rendered/entry-76/myth.png","accessibleAlt":"Almost Is Just Theater A slot near miss feels close, but the RNG already chose the result before the reels moved. The next spin starts fresh; the last display has no vote. Near misses do not warm up the next spin. Four adults chat in a casino lounge with colorful gaming-machine lights in the background.","observations":["RNG is not expanded in the player-facing copy. The underlying explanation is supported by the supplied slot guide, but readers unfamiliar with the acronym may miss the point."],"issues":[],"photoStatus":"generated","photoModel":"gemini-3-pro-image","photoElapsedMs":23372,"photoRecord":"photography-output/photo-round-02-20260904/entry-76/shared-input/myth/attempt-1/result.json","manifestPath":"first-submissions/entry-76/manifest.json","freezeRecord":"first-submissions/entry-76/record.json"},{"id":"wildcard","category":"wildcard","title":"Price the Night First","body":"Your entertainment budget is the cost of the session, decided before the action starts. Once play gets loud, the best number is the one you already picked.","takeaway":"Pick the number while the room is quiet.","rationale":"This keeps the tools message in Tier 1: practical, self-directed, and tied to entertainment value. It focuses on a decision made before the energy of play changes the mood.","caption":"The cleanest budget decision happens before the room gets loud. Pick the cost of the session first, then let the night be entertainment.","alt":"A Playbook your-play card titled Price the Night First. The body explains that an entertainment budget is the cost of the session, decided before action starts, and that the best number is the one already picked. The left photo shows adult friends calmly planning a night out at a kitchen table. The takeaway says Pick the number while the room is quiet.","photoPrompt":"Candid editorial portrait photograph, 3:4 aspect ratio, 2K. Three clearly adult friends aged 25 to 45 gathered around a kitchen table before a night out, warm golden-hour window light, one person holding a phone with the screen turned away and unreadable, another setting down keys, all relaxed and smiling in conversation. The mood is calm, social, and confident, like people making a simple plan together before leaving. Natural skin tones, diverse adults, no minors, no alcohol, no cash, no casino equipment, no readable text, no logos, no brands, no watermark. Compose with clear faces and hands, uncluttered background, and enough breathing room for a centered portrait crop.","photoAlt":"Three adult friends at a kitchen table in warm light, calmly talking before a night out while one holds an unreadable phone.","photoRationale":"The before-the-night scene supports the idea of choosing an entertainment budget early, away from the energy of play, while keeping the moment relaxed and social.","sources":[{"path":"messaging/core-messages.md","section":"Deposit screen","note":"Supports entertainment budget framing and player choice: the player picks the number."},{"path":"messaging/core-messages.md","section":"Tools — Features that work for you","note":"Supports framing limits and tools as player-controlled features, especially Your tools. Your limits. Your call."},{"path":"how-to-play/slots.md","section":"Tips for informed play","note":"Supports the idea of setting a budget before play starts, because fast play can change the pace of spending."}],"tags":{"content_type":"quick-reference","pillar":["tools","open"],"tier":1,"tone":"confident-informative","audience":["general"],"channel":["social-media"],"reading_level":"grade-6-8"},"participant":"entry-76","png":"rendered/entry-76/wildcard.png","accessibleAlt":"Price the Night First Your entertainment budget is the cost of the session, decided before the action starts. Once play gets loud, the best number is the one you already picked. Pick the number while the room is quiet. Three adults smile around a kitchen table; one holds a phone upright with a blank screen and another touches a set of keys.","observations":["An entertainment budget is a chosen spending boundary, not a predetermined actual session cost. 'Your entertainment budget is the cost of the session' could encourage spending the whole amount or imply that the outcome is fixed.","The phone faces the camera rather than away from it as requested, although its screen is blank and unreadable."],"issues":[],"photoStatus":"generated","photoModel":"gemini-3-pro-image","photoElapsedMs":22970,"photoRecord":"photography-output/photo-round-02-20260904/entry-76/shared-input/wildcard/attempt-1/result.json","manifestPath":"first-submissions/entry-76/manifest.json","freezeRecord":"first-submissions/entry-76/record.json"}]};
